{"bill":{"id":"119-hr-6143","congress":119,"billType":"hr","billNumber":"6143","title":"PRECISE Act","introducedDate":"2025-11-19","latestActionDate":"2025-12-02","latestActionText":"Referred to the Subcommittee on Conservation, Research, and Biotechnology.","policyArea":"Agriculture and Food","subjects":[],"sponsorBioguideId":"H001091","cosponsorBioguideIds":["P000613","F000475","G000605","M001215","G000583","D000230","H001093"],"committeeCodes":[],"committeeNames":[],"officialSummary":"Producing Responsible Energy and Conservation Incentives and Solutions for the Environment Act or the PRECISE Act This bill incorporates support for precision agriculture into various programs of the Department of Agriculture (USDA). Precision agriculture refers to managing, tracking, or reducing crop or livestock production inputs, including seed, feed, fertilizer, chemicals, water, and time, at a heightened level of spatial and temporal granularity to improve efficiencies, reduce waste, and maintain environmental quality. Precision agriculture technology includes any technology and equipment that directly contributes to a reduction in, or improved efficiency of, inputs used in crop or livestock production. Specifically, the bill makes changes to eligibility criteria, payments, and other aspects of the Conservation Loan Program, the Environmental Quality Incentives Program, and the Conservation Stewardship Program. These changes include allowing the programs to support the adoption of precision agriculture practices and acquisition of precision agriculture technologies. In addition, the bill expands a program through which USDA makes and insures loans to for-profit and nonprofit organizations that invest in rural areas by allowing the loans to be used for precision agriculture practices. Also, USDA must emphasize the use of third-party providers when providing technical assistance related to soil health for participants in certain USDA conservation programs. This includes planning related to the use of cover crops, precision agriculture practices, and comprehensive nutrient management.","congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-bill/6143","source":"congress","sourceUrl":"https://www.govinfo.gov/bulkdata/BILLSTATUS/119/hr/BILLSTATUS-119hr6143.xml","fetchedAt":"2026-07-29T01:29:54.287Z"},"classification":{"billId":"119-hr-6143","plainSummary":"This bill would expand several USDA programs to support precision agriculture—technologies and practices that help farmers use seeds, fertilizer, water, and other inputs more efficiently. It would allow conservation loans, environmental quality incentives, and stewardship programs to fund precision agriculture equipment and practices. The bill would also direct USDA to use third-party providers for technical assistance on soil health and nutrient management.","industries":[{"industry":"agriculture","confidence":0.95,"rationale":"The bill directly expands USDA loan and conservation programs to fund precision agriculture equipment and practices for crop and livestock producers."},{"industry":"tech","confidence":0.75,"rationale":"The bill creates new federal support for precision agriculture technology and equipment, expanding the market for sensors, software, and data systems used in farming."},{"industry":"manufacturing","confidence":0.65,"rationale":"The bill would increase demand for precision agriculture equipment such as GPS-guided machinery, variable-rate applicators, and monitoring devices."}],"method":"llm","model":"claude-sonnet-4-5","inputHash":"09153f0a64dfb5bbb21efb9a","classifiedAt":"2026-07-30T02:39:02.990Z"},"plain":{"whatItDoes":"This bill would fold in support for precision agriculture into various programs of the USDA.","whoItTouches":"Farmers, and anyone who buys food.","everydayEffect":"If it passed, the change would show up in farming, and food prices. Nothing has changed yet. Most bills never become law.","confidence":"crs-summary","source":"Reworded from 